WEBVTT

00:00:00.694 --> 00:00:07.617
Three cameras. One of them has an incident. Can you spot it before the AI does?

00:00:13.117 --> 00:00:19.070
The gym camera. SafeGuard flagged it automatically, with nobody watching.

00:00:19.783 --> 00:00:29.826
This is SafeGuard, a real-time AI surveillance console I built for my final year project at the University of Hertfordshire. Here is everything it does.

00:00:30.909 --> 00:00:43.462
Every camera is captured once and analysed once, however many people are watching. A trained classifier scores five second windows of motion, and a separate model hunts for weapons.

00:00:44.006 --> 00:00:51.909
When something fires, the incident feed updates instantly, and the alert shows why it fired, not just a score.

00:00:55.597 --> 00:01:02.435
Every alert keeps a snapshot, a short video from before and after, and the cues behind the decision.

00:01:02.952 --> 00:01:11.235
An operator acknowledges it, and one click exports an evidence report: the snapshot hash, the reasoning, and who handled it.

00:01:20.035 --> 00:01:26.534
The weapon detector watches the same streams. Here it catches a rifle, then a knife.

00:01:41.777 --> 00:01:49.110
Analytics show alert trends, busiest hours, hot spot cameras and how quickly operators respond.

00:01:49.618 --> 00:01:53.991
You can also upload a recorded video and let SafeGuard scan it.

00:01:56.182 --> 00:02:01.035
It returns an incident timeline, the peak risk and evidence frames.

00:02:01.644 --> 00:02:11.452
Face search ranks a photo against the enrolled watchlist and opens the full case file. Faces are blurred in this demo, and every record is fictional.

00:02:11.961 --> 00:02:20.364
On a fifty four person test, the right person ranked first ninety eight percent of the time, with each photo held out of the gallery.

00:02:21.098 --> 00:02:25.152
Quick quiz. Is this website safe to pay?

00:02:31.523 --> 00:02:40.176
Not safe. Thirteen complaints are on record. Anyone can check a business or website here, with no login.

00:02:40.687 --> 00:02:48.705
A near miss spelling gets a careful warning. An unknown name says no complaints found, which is not the same as safe.

00:02:53.976 --> 00:02:59.994
The public can report a business too, but a tip only counts after a staff member approves it.

00:03:10.006 --> 00:03:13.125
Approved, and the check now flags it.

00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:24.671
Under the hood: hashed passwords, CSRF protection, rate limits, audit logging, automatic data retention, and eighty three automated tests.

00:03:25.186 --> 00:03:36.773
And I measure honestly. The violence model scores seventy nine percent recall and eighty one percent precision on four hundred held out clips, and the README documents where it fails.

00:03:37.282 --> 00:03:46.950
That is SafeGuard. The code and documentation are on GitHub. Which feature would you build next? Tell me in the comments.
